Appendix. Build and test dependencies, which no image contains

The sections above cover everything a redistributed image carries. The
tools below run on a development or CI host, produce no bytes that reach
a board, and are redistributed by nobody here; they are named so that a
contributor knows what running the test tiers fetches and under which
terms.

tools/renode/fetch-renode-rp2040.sh clones matgla/Renode_RP2040 at a
pinned commit into tools/renode/vendor, which .gitignore excludes.
That project supplies the RP2040 peripheral models Renode itself does
not ship. Its C# models are under the MIT license, copyright Mateusz
Stadnik, with parts derived from Antmicro's Renode peripherals, also
MIT. It carries a built image of the RP2040 boot ROM under the license
of raspberrypi/pico-bootrom-rp2040; the emulator loads that image so
that the kernel's rom_func_lookup() in sys/arch/rp2040/dev/flash.c
resolves against real boot ROM machine code rather than a stub.

tools/pico-sdk/fetch-pico-sdk.sh clones raspberrypi/pico-sdk at a pinned
commit into tools/pico-sdk/vendor, which .gitignore excludes, and
initializes its lib/tinyusb submodule. The SDK is BSD 3-Clause, copyright
Raspberry Pi (Trading) Ltd., and TinyUSB is MIT, copyright Ha Thach. Only
the flash-id probe in tools/flash-id builds against them; no byte of
either reaches a board through this tree, because the root manifest does
not carry the probe. The second-stage boot code that
does reach a board is section 4 above, carried in the tree under its own
notice.

Renode, the arm-none-eabi toolchain, qemu, bmake, shellcheck, ruff and
the Python packages distrib/rp2040/host depends on are installed by a
package manager under their own terms and are named in
sys/arch/rp2040/doc/TESTING.md beside the tier that needs each.
